listening to that interview and the more  serious elements of it as well was a  labor she had a business secretary  Rebecca at Long Bailey she's in her  constituency in Sulphur for us this  morning thank you for being on the  program at miss Long Bailey well we just  heard from Boris Johnson who said that  if Jeremy Corbyn is Prime Minister after  the selection then we're gonna have two  referendums one and brexit and one on  Scottish independence is he right no I  mean what we've said is that were taking  a sensible approach after three years of  what have been quite shambolic  negotiations and the conduct of the  Conservative Party which has turned on  itself what we've said is that will  renegotiate a deal a sensible deal with  the European Union that puts our economy  working life and standards first and we  will put that deal to a public vote so  that people can decide whether that was  what they voted for and whether they're  happy to accept it so that is a  referendum then isn't it it's well it's  a referendum of sorts it's certainly not  a rerun of the original referendum I  think the position of that referendum is  perfectly clear where we are now is  determining whether the country is happy  with the final deal and they will have  their say in that final vote so in that  referendum that you are promising  will you campaign for leave will remain  well we'll make an assessment of the  deal at the time we'll try and get the  best deal that we possibly can we've set  out clearly what our negotiating  strategy would be a customs union strong  single market alignment a floor under  rights and protections and standards and  if we get all of that then we'll have a  conference with our Labour Party members  to determine how we will campaign in  that it could be the case that we get  70% of that and we have to make a  judgement on whether we think that the  final deal is good enough for us to take  forward but ultimately that decision  will be taken at the time so just to be  crystal clear then it seems pretty  obvious from what you're saying that  Labour's campaigning for a second  referendum and in that referendum it is  entirely feasible that labor could  campaign to leave the European Union  well we'll have to make that judgment at  the time based on the deal that we  managed set to Ghana from the European  Union but ultimately our priority has  always been to put our economy fir  to respect the result of the referendum  get the best deal that we possibly can  and ensure that we leave in a way that  doesn't damage our manufacturing  industry that doesn't strip away workers  rights and protections that doesn't take  away our environmental and consumer  standards and that's why we will examine  the final deal very very closely to make  sure that it underpins everything that  we've been setting out for the last  three years so that's the question again  because it's pretty clear you could  campaign to leave the EU in that  referendum that's right isn't it well  that will that will be a decision that  will be taken at the time by our party  we're a democratic party and that's why  we're having a special conference to  determine the final position but  ultimately underpinning our final  decision will be how good that deal is  we will try to get the most credible and  economically supportive deal that we  possibly can so that the British people  have the opportunity to choose upon a  deal that's credible that they will as  opposed to a deal that they've got at  the moment from Boris Johnson which  would strip away workers rights which  isn't economically credible we want to  give them the full choice and the  question at the beginning as well was  also talking about a Scottish  independence referendum could you rule  out a Labour government ever granting  another Scottish independence referendum  well it's certainly not something that  we're advocating under any circumstances  what we want to Scotland to be part we  want Scotland to be part of Great  Britain we think that we're fantastic  partners but ultimately what we have  said is that after the next Scottish  government elections if the Scottish  government determined that they want to  pursue another referendum and they go  through the legislative process within  their own government to push that  forward then there's a government we  wouldn't stand in their way we wouldn't  try and stop it but we certainly  wouldn't become painting to advocate  that Scotland leaves the United Kingdom  at all okay and interesting stuff and  pretty clear onto there I think and now  I'm keen to talk about your policy  announcement today because labour is  saying that if you win the next election  you want to insulate every home in the  United Kingdom just tell me exactly what  it is that you're proposing yes it's  well it's the largest upgrade to UK  housing since the post-war period  and it's not just insulating homes it's  also introducing new renewable  technologies like he solar so that you  could heat your hot water with solar  panels for example and other renewable  technologies and what we'd be doing was  offering that opportunity to every  single home in the UK we estimate that  it would cut at cases of asthma by five  hundred thousand by 2030 it would  eliminate fuel poverty by the mid-2020s  Engel household bill would go down and  the interesting part because ultimately  you will be wanting to talk about costs  there is no upfront costs to anybody who  wants to take part in this scheme those  who are on low-income salaries or or  incomes they will not have to pay a  penny they will receive a grant to be  able to carry out this work on their  homes those who are able to pay they  won't pay any upfront costs they will  have decreasing bills and a small  portion of that decreasing bill will be  used in the long term to pay for the  work that's been carried out so nobody  will be out-of-pocket as part of this  scheme you're right I do want to talk  about the money and it's interesting  that you say there's going to be no  upfront cost to the individuals which  may be right but there clearly are going  to be upfront costs when you have to pay  for this you have to pay for the people  who come into the houses to do the work  you have to pay for materials and those  upfront costs have to be met by somebody  and I have to say when I looked at the  figures for this it is quite staggering  I mean this policy is going to cost you  estimate 250 billion pounds you're gonna  provide 60 billion pounds of government  funds now that's 50% more than the  entire defense budget it's two thirds of  the size the Department for Education  annual budget I mean how on earth you're  gonna find that money well 60 billion  will be the direct capital government  investment that will come out of our  national transformation I'm talking  about down of the to the river well the  remainder of the 250 will be civilian  first I just want to talk about the 60  billion fast because the 60 billion  pounds is an awful lot of money and I'm  asking you where you're gonna get it  from so it will come out of our national  transformation fund which is the 250  billion pot of capital investment  expenditure that John McCauley sets out  at the last general election  so borrowing so yes it would be but  ultimately what we've factored into all  of this we've done financial modeling  that shows that that sixty billion will  be the government's outlay in the  initial stages but overall we're looking  at generating over four hundred thousand  jobs and supporting businesses  throughout the economy so by 2030 the  increased tax take v80 corporation tax  national insurance income tax more than  recoup that sixty billion pounds outlay  in fact it creates a surplus the  interest-free loans as I say that will  represent the remainder of the  expenditure and that will be repaid over  time through savings on bills themselves  and so that's the sixty billion pounds  one hundred and ninety billion pounds of  it is going to come from energy savings  just explain to me how that's going to  work because clearly energy savings will  only come over time it could be a few  years until you managed to get that  money back so bills will go down in  every home will be different some homes  will need double glazing some homes will  only need loft insulation some homes  will need the full suite of things that  are on offer from floor insulation to  loft insulation to cavity wall  insulation to all of the renewable  technologies that were installing to  bring you know heating bills down and  bills will go down very very quickly and  there will be a portion of that bill  that are saving if you like that will be  allocated to repaying the interest-free  loan the rest of the saving will be  enjoyed by the bill pay so they won't be  paying anything extra at all on top of  what they're paying now I'll be  perfectly clear about that okay now  while we're talking about money and la  4mp this week at Lloyd Ross and Moyles  said and I don't think that anyone in  this country should be a billionaire do  you agree with it  well I think in terms of where our  economies are at the moment we're seeing  inequality increasing on an  unprecedented scale a group called the  economic justice commission a few years  ago brought out a report into prosperity  and economic justice within the UK that  was made up of experts right across the  UK academics and business leaders  who stated very very clearly that our  economy was fractured and unless we  change the way that we operate it the  economy wasn't sustainable going forward  and we were not going to address the  inequality question so what we need to  see happening and this is certainly a  central pillar of Labor's economic  offering is a rebalancing of our economy  and that goes all the way through from  investing in areas that haven't seen  investment for many decades rebalancing  the way our economy works in terms of  corporate governance installing workers  on boards for example so the executive  remuneration and the pay of workers is  increased so that that's fairer it's  also about making sure that our benefit  system and Social Security is fit for  purpose and it's also about making those  critical investments in industrial  strategy that improve productivity and  our green industrial revolution is one  central pillar of that a huge economic  lever that we're going to use to  kick-start the economy and deal with the  climate crisis the same time that's very  different isn't it talking about the  need to rebalance the economy and saying  nobody in the whole of the country  should be a billionaire particularly  when you take into account that the top  1% of earners actually paid 27 percent  the country's income tax I mean too many  people listening they'll think this just  sounds like the politics of envy it's  not the politics of envy I think that  Lloyd Russell Moyer was highlighting the  staggering inequality that we see in  Great Britain that makes many people  angry there's nothing wrong with working  hard and certainly we advocate that you  work hard do well but ultimately we've  got a moral responsibility all of us to  pay tax and to improve the communities  in which we live but we also can't be  within an economy that sees people  sleeping on the streets and homeless  those who are going to food banks just  to receive food many of whom are  actually in paid work at the moment and  think we must recognize that there are  fundamental problems in our economy that  have to be addressed of course we want  people to do well and we want to  celebrate that but we also need to  recognize that something fundamentally  has to change so that we're not seeing  this inequality which quite frankly is  akin to Victorian times at the moment  and just very quickly John McDonnell  says he wants the next leader of the  a party to be a woman you been lined up  well at the moment we are getting Jeremy  Corbyn elected as prime minister so that  on December 12 we can absolutely  transform our economy and deal with the  inequality that we've just been talking  about but not only that offer a  transformational program that offers  hope for the future looks at a green  industrial revolution that will upgrade  every single home and usher in a new era  of public luxury that we haven't seen  for many many decades and that's quite  exciting I'm excited about it and I'll  be doing everything I can in this  election campaign to make sure that that  happens okay thank you very much for  going to show apologies if there were  any audio issues there thanks very much

